# supabase-backup.env — the one secret server/supabase-backup.sh needs.
#
# Install:  sudo cp server/supabase-backup.env.example /etc/supabase-backup.env
#           sudo chown root:root /etc/supabase-backup.env && sudo chmod 600 /etc/supabase-backup.env
#           sudo nano /etc/supabase-backup.env   # fill in the real value
#
# ⛔ SUPABASE_DB_URL IS THE POSTGRES SUPERUSER. It bypasses every RLS policy
# over real student data (.claude/rules/security.md). This file must be
# root-owned, mode 600, and never copied to a laptop, a VITE_ var, or
# anything under src/. It is the SAME credential `.env.local`'s
# SUPABASE_DB_URL entry would hold on a laptop running tools/apply-migration.mjs
# — this is the VM's OWN copy, not a reference to that one.
#
# Try the DIRECT host first:
#   postgresql://postgres:<password>@db.<ref>.supabase.co:5432/postgres?sslmode=require
#
# If this VM has no IPv6 egress (test: `curl -6 https://ifconfig.co`, or
# `dig +short db.<ref>.supabase.co AAAA` — a direct host with no AAAA record
# IS the production ref having one, this is about the VM's OWN route out),
# the direct host will time out, not reject the password — see this script's
# own header for why that failure mode is easy to mis-diagnose as a wrong
# credential. Switch to the SESSION POOLER string instead (Supabase
# dashboard → Project Settings → Database → "Connection pooling" → copy the
# "Session mode" string — the pooler listens on IPv4 by design, which is
# the whole reason it exists):
#   postgresql://postgres.<ref>:<password>@<pooler-host>:5432/postgres?sslmode=require
#
# UNVERIFIED FROM THIS AGENT (no VM access, no production DB credential here)
# — whoever installs this must run ONE backup by hand first
# (`sudo -u ubuntu server/supabase-backup.sh`) and read the exit code before
# trusting the timer. skills/backup-and-export.md has the full checklist.
SUPABASE_DB_URL=postgresql://postgres:CHANGE_ME@db.CHANGE_ME.supabase.co:5432/postgres?sslmode=require

# Where backups land and how many nights are kept (defaults shown; both optional).
# BACKUP_DIR=/var/backups/supabase
# BACKUP_KEEP=14
